## What Changed (Post-DI Reorganization)

The Embassy breach was a systemic failure. The Thalmor response was not panic — it was consolidation. Assets recalled, networks restructured, authority redistributed. Alonythil's promotion is part of this: competent handlers moved up to fill the gaps Elenwen's overreach created.

For Alenawe, this means:
- Her handler is no longer *hers*. He's managing a region. She's one of many.
- Her reports fed upward into a model she doesn't control and can no longer shape.
- The institution has already decided what the Dragonborn is — based on data she provided. Data she knows is incomplete. Data she filtered. Data she was already shaping before she admitted to herself she was doing it.
- The confidence the Thalmor have in their Dragonborn assessment is built on her compromise. They just don't know it.

## Why the Reports are a Red Herring

The old frame: "Your reports are contradictory. Explain."
The new frame: "Your reports were excellent. You're done."

The reports worked. That's the problem.

They were consistent, professional, well-structured. They painted a picture of a volatile but manageable actor. The Thalmor read them and concluded: we understand this person well enough. The risk of maintaining an embedded asset in proximity to a potential enemy of the state outweighs the marginal intelligence value of continued observation.

They're right. By every institutional metric, they're right.

But WE know — and she knows — that the reports are incomplete. She's been omitting. Not lying. Omitting. The model they built from her data has gaps she put there. Some deliberately. Some because she didn't know how to file what she was observing. Some because filing it honestly would have required admitting things she wasn't ready to admit.

The Thalmor's rational, correct decision to pull her out is based on a model she already compromised. And the only person who knows the model is wrong is the person who broke it.

She can't say "your model is incomplete" without explaining WHY. And explaining why means explaining what she left out. And explaining what she left out means explaining who she's becoming.

