## Why This Frame is Stronger (Design Notes)

**She argues against success, not suspicion.**
The old frame puts her on trial — "explain the discrepancy." The new frame offers her an exit — "you've done well, come home." Refusing a reward is more revealing than surviving an interrogation. You don't turn down institutional recognition unless something has fundamentally shifted inside you.

**Alonythil is more dangerous distracted.**
As personal handler, his full attention on her case says "you matter." As regional coordinator, his partial attention says "you are one of many." The institutional intimacy is gone. When he DOES focus — when she says something that catches him — that attention weighs more because it's chosen, not obligated. And the detail that she's the stop he lingered on longest, that neither of them will name — that's where his humanity lives.

**The reports become ironic.**
The Thalmor are confident because her reports were good. But WE know the reports were filtered. The institution's model of the Dragonborn is built on data she already compromised. Their rational decision to pull her out is based on a picture she painted incompletely. The only person who can explain why the model is wrong is the person who broke it — and explaining that means explaining everything.

**The gameplay justification is organic.**
She stays not because she won an argument, but because Alonythil runs a calculation: during an active existential crisis, extracting the only embedded asset with direct personal access to the Dragonborn is more expensive than the risk of compromise. The institution doesn't agree. It calculates. The leash gets longer. For now. That's honest — and it preserves tension for MajQ2, where the system comes back to check whether her calculation was worth it.

**The Force Greet is more interesting.**
Old: "Explain why we should risk her on you." (Defensive — the player justifies.)
New: "You know what she is. You brought her here. Why?" (Diagnostic — the player reveals.)
The old version asks the player to make a case. The new version asks the player to be honest. What they say tells Alonythil — and Alenawe — what this relationship actually is. The player isn't defending a position. They're exposing one.
